{
  "title": "TKA Implant Fixation",
  "url": "https://www.orthobullets.com/recon/423327/tka-implant-fixation",
  "subtitle": "Updated: Feb 14 2026",
  "sections": [
    {
      "text": "Summary",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "-1",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "TKA fixation is achieved via two primary methods: Cemented (Polymethylmethacrylate/PMMA) or Cementless (Biologic fixation/Press-fit).",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "text": "Goal is to provide stable mechanical interface between implant and bone to transfer loads and prevent loosening.",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "text": "Cemented fixation remains the gold standard with the longest track record of survivorship.",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "text": "Cementless fixation is increasing in popularity, particularly for younger, active patients, driven by advances in porous metal technology (e.g., highly porous titanium, tantalum).",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Biomechanics & Basic Science",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "-1",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Cemented Fixation (PMMA)",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "mechanism: mechanical interlock.",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "cement does not adhere/bond to bone; it interdigitates into the trabecular bone lattice.",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "-1",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "biomechanics:",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "acts as a grout to distribute loads evenly.",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "-1",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "Young’s Modulus is between cortical and cancellous bone (allows load transfer).",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "-1",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "key technical factors for success:",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "cement penetration",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "-1",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "ideal interdigitation depth is 3–5 mm into cancellous bone.",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "-1",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "text": "pulse Lavage",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "-1",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "crucial to remove blood/fat and open trabecular pores for cement penetration.",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "-1",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Cementless Fixation (Biologic)",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "mechanism",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "osseointegration (Bone ingrowth or ongrowth).",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "-1",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "requirements for ingrowth",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "micromotion",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "-1",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "must be minimized",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "-1",
                      "children": [
                        {
                          "text": "Micromotion > 150 microns leads to fibrous tissue formation instead of bone ingrowth.",
                          "table": null,
                          "images": [],
                          "importance": "-1",
                          "children": []
                        }
                      ]
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "text": "Pore Size",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "-1",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "optimal pore size is 100–400 microns to facilitate vascularization and osteoblast migration.",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "-1",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "text": "Bone Quality",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "-1",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "requires active host bone biology (osteoblasts) and strong trabecular bone for initial press-fit stability.",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "-1",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Surface Technologies:",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "Porous Plasma Spray",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "Titanium particles sprayed onto base.",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "-1",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "Sintered Beads",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "Cobalt-chrome or titanium beads.",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "-1",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "Trabecular Metal (Tantalum)",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "High porosity (~80%) and low modulus of elasticity (closer to bone), potentially reducing stress shielding.",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "-1",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "Hydroxyapatite (HA) Coating",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "Osteoconductive; enhances early gap filling and bone apposition.",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "-1",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Indications",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "-1",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Cemented TKA",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "Elderly patients (> 70 years).",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "Poor bone quality (Osteoporosis/Osteopenia).",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "Inflammatory arthritis (Rheumatoid Arthritis) due to compromised bone stock.",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "Irradiated bone.",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "Revision TKA (often required when metaphyseal bone is compromised).",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Cementless TKA",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "Younger patients (< 65 years).",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "High demand/Active patients.",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "Good bone stock (allows for tight press-fit).",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "Morbid Obesity: Some studies suggest cementless may reduce aseptic loosening rates in high BMI patients compared to cemented (controversial, but gaining evidence).",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Contraindications for Cementless",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "Active infection.",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "Severe osteoporosis (risk of subsidence/fracture).",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "Renal osteodystrophy.",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Technique: Cemented Fixation",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "-1",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Preparation",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "Pulsatile Lavage: Mandatory to remove fat/marrow; increases cement shear strength.",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "Drying: Bone bed must be dry (dry lap sponges, suction) to prevent blood lamination at the interface.",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Cementing Technique",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "Vacuum Mixing: Reduces porosity of cement, increasing fatigue strength.",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "Pressurization: Applying cement early (doughy phase) and pressurizing into the tibia leads to deeper penetration.",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Pros",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "Immediate full weight-bearing stability.",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "Antibiotic delivery (via antibiotic-loaded cement).",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "Forgiving technique (can fill small bone defects/gaps).",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Cons",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "Cement Disease: Risk of thermal necrosis if cement mantle is too thick.",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "Third-body wear: Loose cement particles can accelerate polyethylene wear.",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "Difficult Revision: Removal of well-fixed cement risks massive bone loss.",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Technique: Cementless Fixation",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "-1",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Preparation",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "Precision Cuts: Requires exact bone cuts; gaps > 1–2 mm may prevent ingrowth.",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "Press-Fit: Components are typically oversized (line-to-line or +0.5mm) to generate friction (\"scratch fit\") for initial stability.",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Fixation Augmentation",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "Screws: Often used in tibial baseplates to increase initial stability and reduce lift-off/micromotion.",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "Pegs/Keels: Tibial keel design critical for resisting rotational forces.",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Pros",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "Biological Fixation: Potential for permanent fixation (bone is living, cement is dead).",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "Bone Stock Preservation: Easier to remove implant during revision (often pops off at the interface).",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "OR Efficiency: No waiting for cement to cure (saves ~10–15 mins).",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Cons",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "Cost: Implants are typically more expensive than cemented (though OR time savings may offset this).",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "Thigh Pain: Reported but less common in knees than hips.",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "Incomplete Ingrowth: Risk of fibrous stability leading to late loosening.",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Hybrid Fixation",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "-1",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Definition",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "Cementless Femur + Cemented Tibia.",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Rationale",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "Combines the reliable track record of femoral bone ingrowth (high surface area, good bone quality) with the security of cemented tibial fixation.",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "The Tibia is the weak link in cementless TKA; historically, the tibial component has the highest rate of aseptic loosening in all-cementless constructs.",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Outcomes & Evidence",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "-1",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Survivorship",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "Modern Designs: Recent JAAOS/JBJS literature shows no significant difference in 10-year survivorship between modern highly porous cementless TKA and cemented TKA.",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "Historical Data: Older cementless designs (1980s/90s) had higher failure rates due to poor locking mechanisms (backside wear) and poor patchy ingrowth.",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Complications",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "Aseptic Loosening:",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "Historically higher in cementless tibial components.",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "-1",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "Newer \"highly porous\" metals (e.g., 3D printed Titanium) show loosening rates comparable to cement.",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "-1",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "Radiolucent Lines:",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "More common in cementless tibias initially; non-progressive lines may be normal stress shielding or stable fibrous ingrowth.",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "-1",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Economic Analysis",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "Cementless is cost-effective IF operative time savings > 13 minutes or if revision rate is lower long-term.",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Complications",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "-1",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Surgical Site Infection (SSI)",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "Cemented TKA allows for local antibiotic delivery (Antibiotic Bone Cement), which may lower SSI risk in high-risk patients.",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Periprosthetic Fracture",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "Risk is slightly higher in cementless stems/keels due to the hoop stresses required for press-fit insertion.",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Polyethylene Wear",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "Backside wear (wear between the poly liner and the metal tray) was a historical failure mode of cementless trays (screw holes allowed debris passage). Modern trays effectively seal screw holes or use monoblock designs to prevent this.",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Commonly Used Classification System (Fixation Zones)",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "-1",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Zone 1: Anterior Flange (Femur) / Anterior (Tibia)",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "text": "Zone 2: Anterior Distal (Femur) / Central (Tibia)",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "text": "Zone 3: Posterior Distal (Femur) / Posterior (Tibia)",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "text": "Zone 4: Posterior Flange (Femur)",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "Note: Radiolucencies in continuous zones (e.g., 1 through 4) suggest loosening.",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "gallery": [],
